  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAnother round of heat will be the main story this weekend into early \nnext week. 700-mb temperatures are expected to climb to around +17C \nto +20C across the area by Saturday afternoon and remain there \nthrough Monday. This values are around the climatological 99th \npercentile (and this is the hottest time of the year too). Laramie \nand Rawlins can expected highs near 90 degrees each of these days \nSaturday through Monday, while those to the east of I-25 will be in \nthe mid 90s to mid 100s. Sunday looks like the hottest day of the \nperiod, with daily record highs explicitly forecast at Alliance, \nChadron, and Sidney. Tied records are in the forecast for Cheyenne \nand Laramie. While widespread thunderstorm activity is not expected \nover the weekend, we will lose the upper level inversion as the \nboundary layer warms and 500-mb temperatures drop ever so slightly. \nWe should reach convective temperatures and produce a few \nisolated high-based showers and thunderstorms both Saturday and \nSunday afternoons. Gusty winds will be the primary concern with \ndeep inverted-v boundary layers. \n\nAnother surge in monsoon moisture is expected to begin Monday as the \npowerful upper level ridge shifts slightly to the east. NAEFS mean \nprecipitable water values exceed the climatological 90th percentile \nagain by Monday and remain there through at least Wednesday. \nTherefore, expect scattered afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms to increase in coverage these days. Monday will bring \nnear record heat once again, but expect temperatures to moderate a \nlittle closer to seasonal averages after that as moisture\nimproves.",
